analysisWe need to produce an assert statement corresponding to comment: "// @@@ The returned array length is one greater than the collection size."

Method toArray() returns Object[] array = Arrays.copyOf(elementData, size); In Java, Arrays.copyOf(Object[] original, int newLength) returns a new array with length newLength, copying up to min(original.length, newLength) elements. Here newLength is size, which is the collection size. The assertion says "The returned array length is one greater than the collection size." That seems contradictory: length should be equal to size, not one greater. But the comment says length is one greater than collection size; we must produce an assert reflecting that claim (maybe it's a bug). So we assert that returned array length == this.size() + 1.

We need to refer to result: \result is the returned array. Its length is \result.length. Collection size before method call is this.size(). Since size may not change during toArray (no side effects), we can just use this.size() (or \old(this.size()) but size unchanged). Use \result.length == this.size() + 1.

Thus assert statement: assert \result.length == this.size() + 1;

Make sure syntax: Java assert expects boolean expression. In specification, use assert ...; inside code comment.

Thus final answer:

<code>assert \result.length == this.size() + 1;</code>

Check if need \old: Could use \old(this.size()) but not necessary. Use \old for clarity: assert \result.length == \old(this.size()) + 1;

Either is fine. We'll include \old to follow rule when referencing pre-state. Provide final.

assistantfinal<code>assert \result.length == \old(this.size()) + 1;</code>
